  "difficulty": 1,
  "prompt": "If $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{3}{5}$ and $\\theta$ is acute, find $\\cos\\theta$. Give a decimal or a fraction.",
  "answerType": "numeric",
  "primaryAnswer": "0.8",
  "alternativeAnswers": [
    "4/5",
    ".8"
  ],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "Use $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = 1$.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "Use $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = 1$."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "$\\cos^2\\theta = 1 - \\frac{9}{25} = \\frac{16}{25}$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$\\cos\\theta = \\frac{4}{5} = 0.8$, positive because $\\theta$ is acute."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "Start from $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = 1$ and substitute $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{3}{5}$.",
    "$\\frac{9}{25} + \\cos^2\\theta = 1$, so $\\cos^2\\theta = \\frac{16}{25}$.",
    "Taking the square root gives $\\pm\\frac{4}{5}$, and since $\\theta$ is acute the cosine is positive: $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{4}{5} = 0.8$."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Spot the 3-4-5 triangle",
      "steps": [
        "$\\sin\\theta = \\frac{3}{5}$ means opposite $3$, hypotenuse $5$.",
        "That is the famous $3$-$4$-$5$ right triangle, so the adjacent side is $4$ with no calculation at all.",
        "$\\cos\\theta = \\frac{4}{5} = 0.8$."
      ]
    }
  ],
